1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Subtitles added by Hanson Hu

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:55,000
Episode 19 - I Do

3
00:02:13,420 --> 00:02:14,560
It looks wrong.

4
00:02:16,760 --> 00:02:17,800
Hmmm. It is wrong.

5
00:02:17,860 --> 00:02:19,660
Are they always that difficult to make?

6
00:02:19,720 --> 00:02:21,600
The truth is... yes.

7
00:02:21,880 --> 00:02:24,280
I'll try again.

8
00:02:24,440 --> 00:02:25,160
At this rate,

9
00:02:25,160 --> 00:02:26,780
the wedding will take place tomorrow.

10
00:02:26,780 --> 00:02:28,340
Not to worry.

11
00:02:29,160 --> 00:02:31,120
OK. Here we go.

12
00:02:42,680 --> 00:02:43,600
I'm worried, Philip.

13
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:45,080
What if we can't tie the tie?

14
00:02:45,080 --> 00:02:46,580
If we can't tie the tie,

15
00:02:46,600 --> 00:02:47,560
then there can't be a wedding.

16
00:02:47,560 --> 00:02:48,920
You'd better not make Harry

17
00:02:48,920 --> 00:02:50,760
any more nervous than he is.

18
00:02:51,360 --> 00:02:52,240
Don't worry.

19
00:02:52,240 --> 00:02:52,900
We'll figure a way.

20
00:02:52,900 --> 00:02:54,160
How're you doing, fellas?

21
00:02:54,400 --> 00:02:55,420
Not so good, Grandpa.

22
00:02:55,420 --> 00:02:56,960
We can't get this bow tie tied.

23
00:02:56,960 --> 00:02:58,240
Nobody knows how to do it.

24
00:02:58,240 --> 00:02:58,880
Do you?

25
00:02:59,280 --> 00:03:01,180
No. I never could, either.

26
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:02,680
Well, you have your own tuxedo.

27
00:03:02,680 --> 00:03:03,920
How do you tie your bow tie?

28
00:03:03,920 --> 00:03:04,920
Yeah, Grandpa,

29
00:03:04,920 --> 00:03:05,540
I've seen you in it.

30
00:03:05,540 --> 00:03:06,440
You look great.

31
00:03:06,440 --> 00:03:07,380
How do you tie it?

32
00:03:07,380 --> 00:03:08,960
I don't.

33
00:03:08,960 --> 00:03:09,520
What do you mean?

34
00:03:10,600 --> 00:03:13,460
I never could tie one of those...

35
00:03:13,880 --> 00:03:15,720
things... bow ties.

36
00:03:15,760 --> 00:03:18,440
I have always worn a clip-on bow tie.

37
00:03:18,440 --> 00:03:19,800
A clip-on?

38
00:03:19,800 --> 00:03:20,680
Of course.

39
00:03:20,680 --> 00:03:21,840
Now I remember.

40
00:03:21,840 --> 00:03:23,600
Yes, it's so easy.

41
00:03:23,780 --> 00:03:24,840
All you do is

42
00:03:24,840 --> 00:03:26,980
clip it around under your collar.

43
00:03:26,980 --> 00:03:28,600
We all need one of those.

44
00:03:28,740 --> 00:03:30,800
The tuxedo rental store.

45
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:32,120
Do you think they're open?

46
00:03:32,260 --> 00:03:33,480
Should be.

47
00:03:33,480 --> 00:03:34,680
Sunday's their big day.

48
00:03:34,680 --> 00:03:35,960
I'll call and find out.

49
00:03:36,520 --> 00:03:37,280
Well, if they're not,

50
00:03:37,280 --> 00:03:38,820
I'll lend you mine, Harry.

51
00:03:39,360 --> 00:03:40,560
You're the only one

52
00:03:40,560 --> 00:03:42,960
who really needs to be wearing a tuxedo,

53
00:03:42,960 --> 00:03:43,920
Thanks, Grandpa.

54
00:03:44,240 --> 00:03:45,660
I'm the father of the bride.

55
00:03:45,660 --> 00:03:47,520
I'm supposed to be worried about my daughter,

56
00:03:47,520 --> 00:03:48,440
and here I am with

57
00:03:48,440 --> 00:03:49,720
the man that's marrying my daughter

58
00:03:49,720 --> 00:03:51,040
worrying about him.

59
00:03:51,700 --> 00:03:53,200
Poor Harry.

60
00:03:53,740 --> 00:03:55,520
I know the feeling.

61
00:03:55,760 --> 00:03:57,860
Wedding-day Jitters.

62
00:03:58,880 --> 00:03:59,660
Are they open?

63
00:03:59,660 --> 00:04:00,440
We're in luck.

64
00:04:00,440 --> 00:04:01,120
They're open.

65
00:04:01,120 --> 00:04:03,600
And they have lots of clip-on bow ties.

66
00:04:03,600 --> 00:04:05,800
I'll bicycle down to the village and get them.

67
00:04:05,800 --> 00:04:06,880
You'd better hurry, Robbie.

68
00:04:07,120 --> 00:04:08,940
There's lots of time.

69
00:04:08,940 --> 00:04:10,640
A little over two hours.

70
00:04:13,880 --> 00:04:15,880
In two hours and fifteen minutes

71
00:04:16,180 --> 00:04:17,700
I'll be married to Susan.

72
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:21,440
And be a true member of the Stewart family.

73
00:04:21,720 --> 00:04:23,520
Oh, you're a lucky guy, Harry.

74
00:04:23,700 --> 00:04:27,000
Susan is one of the best women you'll ever find.

75
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:28,640
She's just like her grandma.

76
00:04:28,640 --> 00:04:30,360
Now, once you put the ring on Susan's finger,

77
00:04:30,640 --> 00:04:31,740
you are one of us, Harry.

78
00:04:32,080 --> 00:04:33,840
And don't ever forget it.

79
00:04:35,160 --> 00:04:37,320
Ring? Ring?

80
00:04:38,160 --> 00:04:39,140
Oh my!

81
00:04:40,200 --> 00:04:41,700
What did I do with the rings?

82
00:04:41,840 --> 00:04:45,640
I put them in the pocket of my sports jacket.

83
00:04:45,640 --> 00:04:46,380
No,

84
00:04:46,380 --> 00:04:47,160
I think you put them

85
00:04:47,160 --> 00:04:48,600
in your tuxedo jacket pocket.

86
00:04:48,600 --> 00:04:49,200
Remember?

87
00:04:49,200 --> 00:04:49,680
Right.

88
00:04:56,780 --> 00:04:58,200
What did I do with the rings?

89
00:04:58,580 --> 00:04:59,840
Didn't you give them to Richard?

90
00:04:59,840 --> 00:05:00,800
He's your best man.

91
00:05:01,400 --> 00:05:02,680
I remember.

92
00:05:02,680 --> 00:05:04,000
You gave them to Richard.

93
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:04,800
Oh, yeah.

94
00:05:04,800 --> 00:05:05,760
I remember now.

95
00:05:05,760 --> 00:05:06,900
You handed them to me.

96
00:05:07,640 --> 00:05:08,940
What did I do with them?

97
00:05:08,940 --> 00:05:10,240
I hope they aren't lost.

98
00:05:10,360 --> 00:05:11,860
Oh, don't worry, Harry.

99
00:05:11,860 --> 00:05:13,740
They have to be here.

100
00:05:14,060 --> 00:05:15,200
I remember.

101
00:05:15,320 --> 00:05:17,060
I gave them to Robbie to hold

102
00:05:17,060 --> 00:05:18,120
so I wouldn't lose them.

103
00:05:18,200 --> 00:05:19,020
Where's Robbie?

104
00:05:19,020 --> 00:05:20,360
Relax, Harry.

105
00:05:20,360 --> 00:05:21,760
Robbie went to pick up

106
00:05:21,760 --> 00:05:23,280
the clip-on bow ties.

107
00:05:23,400 --> 00:05:23,840
Oh, I forgot.

108
00:05:23,840 --> 00:05:25,120
What time is it?

109
00:05:25,420 --> 00:05:27,760
It's still a little over two hours, Harry.

110
00:05:27,840 --> 00:05:29,100
Just relax.

111
00:05:29,100 --> 00:05:30,280
What about the rings?

112
00:05:30,280 --> 00:05:32,200
I'm sure Robbie has them.

113
00:05:36,940 --> 00:05:38,080
No, no, Robbie doesn't have them.

114
00:05:38,240 --> 00:05:40,260
He gave them to me to hold on to them

115
00:05:40,640 --> 00:05:42,820
because he didn't want the responsibility of holding them.

116
00:05:43,560 --> 00:05:44,960
I put them in my tuxedo,

117
00:05:44,960 --> 00:05:48,420
but in the lapel pocket.

118
00:05:52,500 --> 00:05:53,360
That's a relief.

119
00:05:54,820 --> 00:05:56,040
I was really worried.

120
00:05:56,040 --> 00:05:57,980
I'll hold on to them for you.

121
00:05:57,980 --> 00:05:59,920
The best man always keeps the rings.

122
00:06:00,180 --> 00:06:00,640
You're right.

123
00:06:00,640 --> 00:06:02,700
You hold on to them so there won't be a problem later.

124
00:06:03,080 --> 00:06:03,480
Well,

125
00:06:03,480 --> 00:06:05,200
now, I think we'd better get dressed, fellas.

126
00:06:05,200 --> 00:06:06,400
All right!

127
00:06:06,400 --> 00:06:07,820
Robbie will bring the ties back.

128
00:06:08,120 --> 00:06:10,260
Hey, leaving me?

129
00:06:10,320 --> 00:06:12,160
You'll be fine.

130
00:06:12,160 --> 00:06:14,000
Try to take it easy.

131
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:15,800
It'll all be over in two hours.

132
00:06:16,160 --> 00:06:16,680
Over?

133
00:06:16,680 --> 00:06:19,880
The wedding ceremony will be over.

134
00:06:20,180 --> 00:06:21,620
You'll be husband and wife.

135
00:06:21,620 --> 00:06:22,660
I guess you're right.

136
00:06:23,140 --> 00:06:24,400
Two hours from now.

137
00:06:36,480 --> 00:06:37,540
Two hours from now.

138
00:06:54,540 --> 00:06:55,840
Two more hours.

139
00:06:56,420 --> 00:06:58,240
Well, that's a long time to wait.

140
00:06:59,160 --> 00:07:00,460
Grandpa's right.

141
00:07:00,460 --> 00:07:02,720
I do have the wedding-day jitters.

142
00:07:03,120 --> 00:07:04,480
I'm nervous.

143
00:07:06,080 --> 00:07:07,560
Why am I so nervous?

144
00:07:08,240 --> 00:07:11,700
I couldn't find the rings, but they were in my pocket.

145
00:07:12,280 --> 00:07:13,640
And now Richard's got them.

146
00:07:14,240 --> 00:07:16,680
He's my best man. He'll take care of them.

147
00:07:17,360 --> 00:07:18,480
Right, right.

148
00:07:18,480 --> 00:07:20,480
I don't need to worry about that.

149
00:07:22,220 --> 00:07:24,120
None of us knew how to tie my bow tie.

150
00:07:24,380 --> 00:07:26,680
But that problem's taken care of now, too,

151
00:07:27,620 --> 00:07:30,920
since Robbie went into the village to pick up some clip-on bow ties.

152
00:07:31,960 --> 00:07:33,540
So why the jitters?

153
00:07:34,840 --> 00:07:35,820
Who am I kidding?

154
00:07:36,920 --> 00:07:38,340
I'm getting married today.

155
00:07:40,100 --> 00:07:40,600
Married!

156
00:07:42,960 --> 00:07:43,780
Married.

157
00:07:45,400 --> 00:07:46,800
In just two hours,

158
00:07:47,380 --> 00:07:51,260
I'll be married to the most wonderful woman in the world.

159
00:07:52,560 --> 00:07:53,900
The woman I love.

160
00:07:55,800 --> 00:07:58,960
I fell in love with Susan on our very first date.

161
00:08:02,720 --> 00:08:03,560
You know something?

162
00:08:04,140 --> 00:08:04,880
What?

163
00:08:05,040 --> 00:08:06,960
I think we're gonna be good friends.

164
00:08:08,720 --> 00:08:09,560
Good night, Susan.

165
00:08:10,320 --> 00:08:11,240
Good night, Harry.

166
00:08:11,380 --> 00:08:12,940
Have a safe trip home.

167
00:08:21,320 --> 00:08:22,320
Are you all right?

168
00:08:24,200 --> 00:08:25,040
Sorry.

169
00:08:26,040 --> 00:08:28,360
I was nervous even then.

170
00:08:29,240 --> 00:08:34,840
Susan is beautiful and intelligent and kind,

171
00:08:34,900 --> 00:08:37,540
and we have fun together.

172
00:08:38,760 --> 00:08:40,600
She'll be a wonderful mother to Michelle,

173
00:08:42,240 --> 00:08:44,320
and soon she'll be my wife,

174
00:08:45,640 --> 00:08:47,320
Mrs. Harry Bennett.

175
00:08:48,540 --> 00:08:50,420
I couldn't be any happier.

176
00:08:51,480 --> 00:08:55,700
Less nervous, maybe, but I couldn't be any happier.

177
00:09:45,320 --> 00:09:46,760
And now for... something old,

178
00:09:46,800 --> 00:09:48,520
something new, something borrowed,

179
00:09:48,520 --> 00:09:49,400
and something blue.

180
00:09:50,060 --> 00:09:51,920
OK. Let's see.

181
00:09:51,960 --> 00:09:53,140
Something borrowed.

182
00:09:54,120 --> 00:09:56,760
That's this dress. Borrowed from Marilyn.

183
00:10:01,520 --> 00:10:02,400
Something blue.

184
00:10:04,420 --> 00:10:06,840
My wristband.

185
00:10:08,600 --> 00:10:11,940
I wore it when I married your father.

186
00:10:18,760 --> 00:10:20,420
Oh, Mother. I forgot you still had it.

187
00:10:20,780 --> 00:10:22,500
It's just so lovely.

188
00:10:22,500 --> 00:10:24,200
I bought it in an antique shop

189
00:10:24,200 --> 00:10:26,160
when I was about eighteen years old.

190
00:10:27,240 --> 00:10:29,480
I saved it for my wedding day.

191
00:10:30,320 --> 00:10:32,680
And you saved it for me, didn't you, Mother?

192
00:10:34,880 --> 00:10:37,620
Yes, honey. I did.

193
00:10:39,320 --> 00:10:41,800
Are we ever going to finish?

194
00:10:47,560 --> 00:10:50,640
All right. OK.

195
00:10:50,640 --> 00:10:53,240
Something borrowed.

196
00:10:53,380 --> 00:10:55,240
The wedding dress. And something blue.

197
00:10:55,240 --> 00:10:57,660
The wristband. Something old?

198
00:10:57,680 --> 00:10:59,180
Something old.

199
00:11:00,240 --> 00:11:01,640
Right. What's old?

200
00:11:01,840 --> 00:11:03,200
Of course!

201
00:11:03,200 --> 00:11:04,260
Something old.

202
00:11:04,260 --> 00:11:06,020
I had planned to wear them.

203
00:11:16,080 --> 00:11:17,500
Grandma's pearls!

204
00:11:17,920 --> 00:11:21,100
Oh, Grandpa will be so pleased that you're wearing them.

205
00:11:22,160 --> 00:11:25,160
I'm sure he misses Grandma on a day like this.

206
00:11:25,780 --> 00:11:27,580
Help me with them, Marilyn.

207
00:11:29,340 --> 00:11:31,160
I've never worn them before.

208
00:11:31,320 --> 00:11:33,180
I've been saving them for today.

209
00:11:33,680 --> 00:11:35,340
Saving them for today?

210
00:11:36,080 --> 00:11:37,640
Oh, you're a real Stewart!

211
00:11:43,380 --> 00:11:44,840
Oh, there you go.

212
00:11:47,820 --> 00:11:48,480
Lovely!

213
00:11:49,880 --> 00:11:50,640
Lovely!

214
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:53,440
Lovely!

215
00:11:56,660 --> 00:11:59,660
Something borrowed, something blue.

216
00:11:59,660 --> 00:12:01,040
Something old...

217
00:12:01,040 --> 00:12:03,600
and now for something new.

218
00:12:03,600 --> 00:12:05,520
The veil.

219
00:12:05,520 --> 00:12:07,080
Oh, it's so beautiful, Marilyn!

220
00:12:08,480 --> 00:12:12,080
You really are a fabulous designer, Marilyn.

221
00:12:27,820 --> 00:12:30,100
Doesn't it look just right on Susan?

222
00:12:30,160 --> 00:12:31,160
Perfect!

223
00:12:32,500 --> 00:12:34,340
And when you both say "I do",

224
00:12:34,740 --> 00:12:38,480
Harry will lift this veil over your head

225
00:12:40,160 --> 00:12:41,360
and kiss the bride.

226
00:12:43,920 --> 00:12:45,360
Oh, I'm so excited!

227
00:12:45,360 --> 00:12:48,260
When you said "I do", Marilyn,

228
00:12:49,040 --> 00:12:51,020
it suddenly became real.

229
00:12:51,620 --> 00:12:53,200
That's all right, Susan.

230
00:12:53,440 --> 00:12:54,800
You've got the wedding-day jitters!

231
00:12:56,480 --> 00:12:58,840
In less than two hours,

232
00:12:58,920 --> 00:13:02,000
you will be Mrs. Harry Bennett.

233
00:13:02,720 --> 00:13:04,760
Oh, that reminds me.

234
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:06,400
If we don't get dressed,

235
00:13:06,600 --> 00:13:07,920
we won't be there

236
00:13:07,920 --> 00:13:11,220
to see Susan become Mrs. Harry Bennett.

237
00:13:11,220 --> 00:13:14,680
Before you leave, do I look all right?

238
00:13:16,720 --> 00:13:18,480
You never looked better.

239
00:13:19,980 --> 00:13:21,120
Mom.

240
00:13:22,620 --> 00:13:24,020
She's right.

241
00:13:26,200 --> 00:13:28,520
And that's dear little Max.

242
00:13:29,400 --> 00:13:30,580
Got to go and feed him.

243
00:13:32,880 --> 00:13:34,600
What am I supposed to do?

244
00:13:35,060 --> 00:13:36,040
Take off the veil,

245
00:13:36,040 --> 00:13:37,680
kick off your shoes, and sit down.

246
00:13:38,900 --> 00:13:40,880
We'll come upstairs and get you in a little while.

247
00:13:41,480 --> 00:13:43,200
Richard's going to take some wedding pictures

248
00:13:43,200 --> 00:13:44,060
before the ceremony.

249
00:13:44,060 --> 00:13:45,120
So just relax.

250
00:13:45,440 --> 00:13:47,940
Are you kidding? Relax?

251
00:14:12,360 --> 00:14:13,760
Susan Stewart...

252
00:14:13,760 --> 00:14:17,840
you are about to become Susan Bennett

253
00:14:36,320 --> 00:14:37,900
Mrs. Harry Bennett.

254
00:14:39,360 --> 00:14:41,180
Soon the wedding will begin.

255
00:14:42,080 --> 00:14:43,040
Am I ready?

256
00:14:45,160 --> 00:14:45,680
Let's see.

257
00:14:45,680 --> 00:14:47,040
I have something old.

258
00:14:47,400 --> 00:14:50,000
The pearls my grandmother gave me.

259
00:14:53,700 --> 00:14:55,480
Something new?

260
00:14:55,960 --> 00:14:57,840
My beautiful veil.

261
00:15:02,080 --> 00:15:03,280
Something borrowed?

262
00:15:03,420 --> 00:15:05,560
This dress from Marilyn.

263
00:15:05,560 --> 00:15:07,120
Oh, it must be lucky.

264
00:15:07,280 --> 00:15:09,760
Marilyn wore this dress when she married Richard,

265
00:15:09,760 --> 00:15:12,880
and they seemed very happy.

266
00:15:13,840 --> 00:15:14,740
Where was I?

267
00:15:14,740 --> 00:15:17,320
Oh, and something blue.

268
00:15:18,080 --> 00:15:20,280
The antique wristband from Mother.

269
00:15:20,820 --> 00:15:22,880
Oh, how sweet.

270
00:15:23,520 --> 00:15:25,720
She saved it for me all these years.

271
00:15:27,720 --> 00:15:28,720
I'm ready.

272
00:15:29,200 --> 00:15:30,460
But I'm nervous.

273
00:15:31,300 --> 00:15:33,880
Are all brides this nervous?

274
00:15:34,280 --> 00:15:37,020
I've waited for this wonderful day all my life.

275
00:15:37,640 --> 00:15:40,400
The day I marry the man I love.

276
00:15:57,280 --> 00:15:59,540
Oh, I hope I'll be a good mother to Michelle.

277
00:15:59,740 --> 00:16:01,880
It was tough for her after her mother died,

278
00:16:03,360 --> 00:16:07,240
but now she's like a daughter to me.

279
00:16:08,760 --> 00:16:09,980
I'm very lucky.

280
00:16:09,980 --> 00:16:14,480
Today I'll become a wife and a mother, all at the same time,

281
00:16:14,480 --> 00:16:17,740
the moment I say "I do".

282
00:16:21,680 --> 00:16:23,640
Susan, will you marry me?

283
00:16:24,280 --> 00:16:25,440
You bet I will.

284
00:16:26,400 --> 00:16:28,080
I love you, Harry.

285
00:17:14,100 --> 00:17:15,940
Philip... Ellen.

286
00:17:15,940 --> 00:17:17,200
Ah, Judge.

287
00:17:17,200 --> 00:17:18,960
How are you?

288
00:17:18,960 --> 00:17:19,400
Hello.

289
00:17:19,400 --> 00:17:21,520
I think it's time for the wedding to begin.

290
00:17:26,960 --> 00:17:28,800
All right, ladies and gentlemen.

291
00:17:28,800 --> 00:17:30,120
Please take your places.

292
00:17:30,200 --> 00:17:32,600
The wedding ceremony is about to begin.

293
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,160
OK, Jane. Start the music.

294
00:18:51,080 --> 00:18:52,420
Dearly beloved,

295
00:18:52,420 --> 00:18:54,340
we are gathered here today

296
00:18:54,340 --> 00:18:59,560
to join this man and this woman in holy matrimony.

297
00:19:00,540 --> 00:19:02,800
Do either of you have any reason

298
00:19:02,880 --> 00:19:05,720
why you should not be legally joined in marriage?

299
00:19:08,060 --> 00:19:10,000
Is there anyone present who can show

300
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,440
any just cause why these two people

301
00:19:12,440 --> 00:19:15,340
should not be legally joined in marriage?

302
00:19:19,360 --> 00:19:22,320
Then, Harry Bennett, do you take Susan Stewart

303
00:19:22,320 --> 00:19:24,080
to be your lawful, wedded wife?

304
00:19:26,580 --> 00:19:27,420
I do.

305
00:19:28,880 --> 00:19:31,040
And you, Susan Stewart,

306
00:19:31,200 --> 00:19:32,540
do you take Harry Bennett

307
00:19:32,540 --> 00:19:34,400
to be your lawful, wedded husband?

308
00:19:36,320 --> 00:19:37,160
I do.

309
00:19:38,040 --> 00:19:39,200
The rings, please.

310
00:19:42,520 --> 00:19:43,520
I have them, Harry.

311
00:20:10,120 --> 00:20:14,320
By the power vested in me by the laws of the State of New York,

312
00:20:14,760 --> 00:20:17,740
I now pronounce you husband and wife.

313
00:20:19,080 --> 00:20:20,940
You may kiss the bride now, Harry.

314
00:20:49,100 --> 00:20:51,160
What a wonderful wedding, Philip.

315
00:20:51,920 --> 00:20:52,600
Just perfect.

316
00:20:54,120 --> 00:20:55,340
It was, Ellen.

317
00:20:55,340 --> 00:20:58,080
And you were the beautiful mother of the bride.

318
00:20:58,480 --> 00:21:00,440
Look at you, here, with Robbie,

319
00:21:00,440 --> 00:21:02,260
as he escorted you onto the patio.

320
00:21:02,640 --> 00:21:04,320
Robbie looked so grown up.

321
00:21:04,760 --> 00:21:06,920
And handsome, like his father.

322
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:09,720
And look at Michelle.

323
00:21:10,200 --> 00:21:11,720
What a sweet flower girl.

324
00:21:11,720 --> 00:21:13,300
She was so excited.

325
00:21:13,300 --> 00:21:14,720
She'd never been to a wedding before.

326
00:21:16,500 --> 00:21:18,840
And here's Susan's bridesmaid, Marilyn.

327
00:21:19,160 --> 00:21:22,060
Marilyn was Susan's matron of honor, Philip.

328
00:21:23,120 --> 00:21:24,960
Look at Richard and Harry.

329
00:21:25,560 --> 00:21:27,520
They seem like brothers, don't they?

330
00:21:28,640 --> 00:21:31,460
And Dad with our new grandson, Max.

331
00:21:31,460 --> 00:21:33,640
I love this picture.

332
00:21:34,580 --> 00:21:38,440
And I love this one of you escorting Susan.

333
00:21:38,440 --> 00:21:40,560
That's our little girl, Ellen.

334
00:21:40,560 --> 00:21:41,120
Oh, Philip.

335
00:21:41,720 --> 00:21:44,360
I remember when she was just a baby.

336
00:21:45,240 --> 00:21:47,460
Our little girl grew up so fast.

337
00:21:55,760 --> 00:22:00,340
And now she's a mature, married woman with her own family.

338
00:22:01,980 --> 00:22:04,160
I feel like I've lost my daughter.

339
00:22:04,180 --> 00:22:06,320
Ellen, you haven't lost a daughter.

340
00:22:07,140 --> 00:22:08,480
We've gained a son.

341
00:22:08,480 --> 00:22:11,520
A talented, intelligent, likable son,

342
00:22:11,720 --> 00:22:14,340
who happens to love our daughter very much.

343
00:22:14,640 --> 00:22:17,720
We also have a lovely new granddaughter, Michelle.

344
00:22:18,720 --> 00:22:20,380
What a wonderful family.

345
00:22:21,160 --> 00:22:22,560
I love you, Philip.

346
00:22:22,920 --> 00:22:24,480
I love you too, Ellen.

